  "theoretical_contribution": "This study introduces a novel theoretical framework that links the interpretability of neurosymbolic large language models (LLMs) to enhanced decision-making outcomes in clinical settings, specifically for diabetes management. It posits that increased interpretability mediates user trust and decision accuracy through psychological mechanisms like perceived transparency.",
  "technical_contribution": "The research develops and validates a new methodology for integrating SHAP and attention visualization techniques into clinical workflows, providing a systematic approach to enhance the interpretability of LLMs in high-stakes environments. This includes the creation of a robust evaluation metric for measuring trust calibration and decision accuracy.",
  "empirical_contribution": "The study empirically validates the effectiveness of interpretable LLMs in real-world healthcare applications, providing quantitative evidence of improved decision accuracy and user trust among healthcare professionals compared to standard LLM outputs. It generates a unique dataset of decision logs and user feedback over a six-month period, contributing to the understanding of AI's role in clinical decision-making.",
  "practical_deployment_contribution": "This research offers concrete deployment guidelines for the integration of interpretable LLMs in diabetes management, addressing ethical compliance and user training needs. It provides actionable insights into how healthcare professionals can effectively utilize AI-driven insights while maintaining trust and transparency in patient care."
}

